#!/usr/bin/env python3.11
"""Drain the in-box issue queue across the whole fleet, unattended.

WHY THIS EXISTS.

The queue at /issues is product-agnostic, but nothing drained it automatically.
`issue-drain` is the only drain mechanism and it is a DAYTIME COST-SHIFT WRAPPER
(ARCH-075): its terminal move is `exec co planning-orchestrator`, an interactive
session, and it validates `case $product in constance|vivy|sous-chef`. So five of
the products with open issues could not be drained even by hand, and none could
be drained on a schedule — looping an interactive co from launchd would open one
session per product. Measured 2026-07-26: 32 open issues across 11 products, of
which the overnight reaches four. Half the issues filed had no reader at all.

This is the unattended half. `issue-drain` keeps its job (a human running a
daytime drain against one product); this command runs nightly across everything.

WHAT IT DISPATCHES. One `planning-orchestrator` per product, non-interactively,
carrying the same end-to-end brief `issue-drain` gives it: claim -> bug-fixer ->
publish -> pr-open -> link_pr -> reviewer + security-reviewer -> auto-cycle ->
record the outcome back on the issue. The orchestration lives in the agent, as it
already does for the overnight; this command decides only WHICH products get one
and refuses the ones it cannot dispatch safely.

WHY state="claimable", NOT state="open". A claim is how a session says "I hold
this" so a drain skips it, and ARCH-361 Part B gave claims a 24h TTL precisely so
a session that dies cannot strand an issue out of the queue forever. The store
exposes that as the pseudo-state `claimable` — open, plus claimed-whose-claim-has-
expired — computed server-side so the TTL lives in one place. `issue-drain`'s
prompt predates all of it and still asks for `state="open"`, which means a run
that dies mid-flight loses every issue it had claimed: the next run cannot see
them. issue_api.py names this exact failure in a comment. Querying `claimable` is
what makes an interrupted pass safe to re-run, which matters most here, because
this is the caller that runs unattended with nobody watching it die.

NO CHECKOUT GUARD. This drain dispatches exactly one agent: `planning-orchestrator`
(see `_ORCHESTRATOR`). `planning-orchestrator` is in `_DEDICATED_WORKTREE_WORKERS`,
so it runs in its own per-task worktree (ARCH-391, 2026-07-29) and never touches the
primary checkout. Workers have always been safe: allocate_worktree cuts their trees
from the local main ref regardless of the primary's state. Issue claims are atomic
server-side (409 on one already taken), so two drains cannot work the same issue.
Write-class workers get dedicated worktrees whose name collisions produce a
WorktreeBusy refusal rather than a race. Because the one agent this drain dispatches
is worktree-class, no checkout guard is needed for this path and none is run.

WHAT IT COSTS, AND WHY IT IS UNBOUNDED ANYWAY. A product's drain spawns a
bug-fixer plus two reviewers PER ISSUE, so a pass over the whole queue is a large
and open-ended spend. That cost is real and unchanged; what changed is the answer
to it.

This shipped bounded at 3 products x 3 issues, which caps an unattended night at
nine issues. Against 11 products and a queue that grows every time a reviewer
files a finding, that does not converge — it is a nightly nibble, and the backlog
outruns it. The operator's call (2026-07-27) is that the nightly clears the queue, so
`--max-products` and `--max-issues` default to 0, meaning every product and every
claimable issue.

The bounds still work; they are now OPT-IN rather than the operating norm. Pass a
number to cap a pass — useful when headroom is short, which is a judgement about
the moment rather than a standing policy. Nothing is lost by stopping early,
because `claimable` re-derives the work from the store on every run.

Usage:
    issue-queue-drain                     # the scheduled pass: everything
    issue-queue-drain --dry-run           # list the plan, dispatch nothing
    issue-queue-drain --product selector  # one product, still guarded
    issue-queue-drain --type bug          # one class of issue
    issue-queue-drain --max-products 3    # bound a pass when headroom is short
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import datetime as dt
import json
import os
import pathlib
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time
import urllib.parse
import urllib.request
from collections import defaultdict

# Every other scheduled dispatcher (e.g. launch-weekly-curation) is a shell
# launcher that exports AGENT_RUN_ORIGIN
# before invoking the Python program, so the budget gate's CAPPED_ORIGINS check
# (ARCH-156) applies to what it spawns. This command has no launcher — the plist
# invokes it directly — so it must set its own default here, or every run
# (including a hand-run for testing, which is autonomous work same as a
# scheduled one) falls to telemetry.DEFAULT_ORIGIN ("interactive") and the
# budget cap never applies to it.
#
# UNCONDITIONAL, not setdefault, and the difference is the whole control.
# AGENT_RUN_ORIGIN is self-asserted and unauthenticated (ADR 0058), and
# `launchctl setenv AGENT_RUN_ORIGIN interactive` — runnable by any process
# under this account, including an autonomous agent holding Bash, which is
# exactly the actor this cap exists to constrain — persists into every
# launchd-started process until it is unset or the machine reboots. A
# setdefault yields to that value, so the next 03:30 run would stamp
# `interactive`, write an otherwise ordinary meta.json, and be silently
# uncapped again with nothing to signal it. The three sibling launchers all
# `export` unconditionally for this reason; this line matches them.
os.environ["AGENT_RUN_ORIGIN"] = "scheduled"

# Prefer this checkout's own lib over the installed farm (bin/knowledge-watcher's
# shape) — a Builder deploy area has bin/ and lib/agent-platform/ together but no
# ~/.local farm at all.
for _libdir in ((pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "lib" / "agent-platform"),
                pathlib.Path.home() / ".local" / "lib" / "agent-platform"):
    if (_libdir / "decision_client.py").is_file():
        sys.path.insert(0, str(_libdir))
        break
import agent_policy as AP  # noqa: E402
import decision_client as DC  # noqa: E402
import issue_gate  # noqa: E402
import registry as R  # noqa: E402
import signal_client as SC  # noqa: E402
import vault_plane  # noqa: E402
from worktrees import is_dispatch_target  # noqa: E402
import builder_root as BR  # noqa: E402

AGENTCTL = pathlib.Path.home() / ".local" / "bin" / "agentctl"

# The knowledge repository whose origin/main holds the policy file, read for the
# review-round cap the brief states (the operator raised it 5 -> 7, 2026-10-01,
# sig_01M3W9600TWE8SVKQNXX6G282A). Same source and the same constant name as
# `registry.KNOWLEDGE_REPO`, so a test points both at one fixture the same way.
KNOWLEDGE_REPO = BR.data_root() / "knowledge"


# The store's list endpoint caps a response at this many rows.
_API_PAGE_CAP = 200

# The queue's issue types (the Library's ITEM_TYPES). Used only to reject a
# mistyped --type at the CLI, loudly: a typo silently matching nothing would
# drain zero issues and report a clean pass, which is the failure mode this
# command was written to stop producing.
_ISSUE_TYPES = {"bug", "nit", "improvement", "task", "epic"}

# Where `planning-orchestrator` is read from, for EVERY product.
#
# The six repo-local crews (hollis, selector, convivy-deck, convivy-services,
# convivy-app-kit, selector-backend) are agentctl's LOCAL_AGENT_REPOS: they
# resolve agents from their own `.claude/agents/`, and not one defines a
# `planning-orchestrator` — their crews are prefixed (sel-*, hollis-*, deck-*,
# svc-*) and selector-backend's directory is empty. Verified 2026-07-26. This is
# the identical trap changelog-drain hit, where it failed 40 of 88 merges; the
# fix is identical too, because `--agents-dir` makes agentctl treat the agent as
# directory-resolved, reading the definition off disk AND skipping knowledge-ctx
# profile activation (selector-backend has no profile mapping, so activation
# would fail before the agent ever ran).
#
# Passed for every product rather than only the broken ones, so this command does
# not carry its own copy of LOCAL_AGENT_REPOS to drift against agentctl's.
# The donors are individuated copies of the same indexed agent agentctl would
# otherwise resolve, so this is a cache rather than a second source of truth.
_ORCHESTRATOR_DONORS = ("vivy", "constance", "sous-chef")
_ORCHESTRATOR = "planning-orchestrator"


def _agents_dir() -> pathlib.Path | None:
    """The directory to resolve the orchestrator from, or None if no donor exists."""
    for repo in _ORCHESTRATOR_DONORS:
        d = BR.root() / repo / ".claude" / "agents"
        if (d / f"{_ORCHESTRATOR}.md").is_file():
            return d
    return None


# A product name that could not be a repo directory. Enforced at INGEST rather
# than at dispatch, because the drain touches the name before agentctl's own
# gate ever sees it (security review, #254):
#
#   * `is_dispatch_target` builds a path from it, and pathlib resolves `..`
#     through the OS — `../sibling-repo` would test a repo outside <root>.
#
# So the drain owns this name and must sanitize it. Same discipline agentctl
# already applies to `--pr` for the same reason (it strips to ASCII digits
# because the value lands in a path and a git ref). The pattern matches
# worktrees._SAFE_SEGMENT, with a leading-dot exclusion so `.` and `..` cannot
# match at all rather than being special-cased afterwards.
_SAFE_PRODUCT = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*$")


def _dispatchable(product: str) -> bool:
    """`agentctl spawn --on <product>` needs a primary checkout (ARCH-391).

    Delegates to the platform's own `is_dispatch_target` rather than re-testing
    for a `.git` path: that function IS agentctl's dispatch test asked as a
    question, so answering it here any other way would let this command disagree
    with what dispatch actually does.
    """
    return is_dispatch_target(product)



def _get(path: str, params: dict) -> list[dict]:
    """One read against the control store. Raises on failure — a drain that cannot
    READ must not report a clean pass over work it never saw."""
    tok = SC.token()
    if not tok:
        raise SystemExit("issue-queue-drain: no ops token; cannot read the queue")
    url = vault_plane.url(f"{path}?{urllib.parse.urlencode(params)}")
    req = urllib.request.Request(url, headers=vault_plane.headers(token=tok))
    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=SC._DELIVERY_TIMEOUT) as resp:
        return json.loads(resp.read().decode("utf-8")).get("items", [])


_SUPERVISOR_SLOT_POLL_SECONDS = 60
_SUPERVISOR_SLOT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 4 * 3600


def _wait_for_supervisor_slot(
    *, poll: int = _SUPERVISOR_SLOT_POLL_SECONDS,
    timeout: int = _SUPERVISOR_SLOT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS,
) -> bool:
    """Block until the registry will admit another supervisor. True when a slot
    opened, False on timeout.

    Asks the registry rather than counting runs here, so the cap lives in exactly
    one place and this cannot drift from it (ARCH-422).

    The timeout is a backstop, not a schedule. A product's drain can legitimately
    run for hours — the agent-platform orchestrator ran 2h14m on 2026-07-29 — so a
    short timeout would drop products that were only ever waiting their turn. Four
    hours from the 01:00 start still lands before the 05:00 healthcheck, and a
    timeout is REPORTED by the caller rather than swallowed, because a product
    silently missing from a night reads as an empty queue.

    Uses time.monotonic so a clock change mid-wait cannot make this return early or
    hang: this runs unattended overnight, across the DST boundary twice a year.
    """
    deadline = time.monotonic() + timeout
    announced = False
    while True:
        ok, _current, reason = R.can_spawn(None, _ORCHESTRATOR)
        if ok:
            return True
        if time.monotonic() >= deadline:
            return False
        if not announced:
            print(f"issue-queue-drain: waiting for a supervisor slot — {reason}",
                  flush=True)
            announced = True
        time.sleep(poll)


def _claimable(types: set[str] | None = None) -> tuple[dict[str, list[dict]], list[str]]:
    """Claimable issues grouped by product, plus warnings worth surfacing.

    `types` narrows to those issue types. Filtered HERE rather than server-side so
    the count of what was excluded is available to report: a type filter that
    silently shrinks the queue is the same failure as a queue with no consumer,
    which is the thing this command exists to have caught.
    """
    warnings = []
    items = _get("/api/issues", {"state": "claimable"})
    if len(items) >= _API_PAGE_CAP:
        # Unlike changelog-drain's entries read, a truncated page here cannot
        # cause duplicate work — the orchestrator claims each issue atomically, so
        # anything this pass does not see is simply picked up next pass. Warn.
        warnings.append(
            f"the queue query returned the {_API_PAGE_CAP}-row cap — this pass sees "
            f"a prefix; the rest are picked up next pass")
    by_product: dict[str, list[dict]] = defaultdict(list)
    rejected = []
    excluded_by_type = 0
    for it in items:
        if types is not None and (it.get("type") or "") not in types:
            excluded_by_type += 1
            continue
        product = it.get("product") or ""
        if not product:
            continue
        if not _SAFE_PRODUCT.match(product):
            # Named, not silently dropped. A rejected row is either an attack or
            # a data defect, and both need a human to see WHICH issue carried it.
            rejected.append(f"{it.get('id') or '?'} ({product!r})")
            continue
        by_product[product].append(it)
    if rejected:
        warnings.append(
            f"dropped {len(rejected)} issue(s) whose product name is not a valid "
            f"repo directory: {', '.join(rejected)}")
    if excluded_by_type:
        warnings.append(
            f"excluded {excluded_by_type} claimable issue(s) by --type "
            f"({','.join(sorted(types or ()))}); they remain in the queue")

    # THE IN-FLIGHT GATE (ARCH-418). `claimable` answers only the CLAIM question —
    # open, plus claims whose TTL expired. It knows nothing about branches or PRs,
    # so an issue whose claim lapsed while its PR stayed open reads as claimable
    # and a drain would dispatch a worker to redo work already in review. That is
    # not hypothetical: it is how the overnight opened convivy/vivy #971 for work a
    # daytime session already had in hand on #944, and it is why issue_gate exists
    # (ARCH-311 moved the decision out of a prompt and into code).
    #
    # This gate previously lived only on launch-overnight's per-product path. That
    # path is being disconnected in favour of this queue-wide drain, so the gate
    # has to come across with it — otherwise consolidating would trade four
    # products' coverage for a duplicate-work regression across eleven.
    #
    # Per product, because the branch scan is a per-repo GitHub API call. eligible_issues
    # already fails CLOSED on a scan failure (it returns everything as skipped), so
    # an unreachable API costs a product one quiet night instead of a duplicate
    # PR — but it must be LOUD here, or a product silently drops out of the drain
    # and looks like an empty queue.
    gated: dict[str, list[dict]] = {}
    for product, group in by_product.items():
        group = _authorized(group, product, warnings)
        if not group:
            continue
        eligible, skipped = issue_gate.eligible_issues(product, items=group)
        if eligible:
            gated[product] = eligible
        for item, reason in skipped:
            warnings.append(
                f"{product}: holding back {item.get('id') or '?'} — {reason}")
    return gated, warnings


def _resolve_decision(decision_id: str) -> tuple[str, str]:
    """Fetch a feature-gate decision; return (state, directive).

    Returns ("error", "") on any failure — fails closed. An authorization gate
    that cannot resolve a decision must hold the issue back, not pass it through.
    """
    if not decision_id:
        return ("error", "")
    result = DC.get_decision(decision_id)
    if result["status"] != "ok" or result["item"] is None:
        return ("error", "")
    item = result["item"]
    state = (item.get("state") or "").strip()
    directive = (item.get("directive") or "").strip()
    return (state, directive)


def _authorized(items: list[dict], product: str, warnings: list[str]) -> list[dict]:
    """Drop `feature` issues nobody authorized (ARCH-403 Tier 1.2, carried here by
    ARCH-418).

    A feature the operator files himself IS the authorization — the plane's successor to his
    🚀 on a Slack message — and the store records that as `created_by_kind ==
    "human"`. An agent-filed feature is a PROPOSAL: it must reach him as a
    feature-gate decision, and must never be built by a drain.

    An agent-filed feature WITH an approved or redirected feature-gate decision IS
    authorized. Both states allow the issue through: `approved` means build as
    proposed; `redirected` means build per the operator's directive (which supersedes the
    original proposal and must reach the orchestrator brief verbatim). Any other
    state — pending, denied, error, or unresolvable — holds the issue back. This
    gate fails CLOSED: a decision lookup failure is NOT an authorization.

    This gate previously existed only in overnight-triage's prompt, which acted on
    human-filed features alone. That prompt is no longer in the loop, and this
    command's orchestrator brief says "work every claimable issue" — so without
    this filter the nightly drain would auto-build every feature an agent proposed.
    That is the authorization gate failing open, unattended, which is worse than
    any duplicate-work case.

    In CODE rather than in the brief, for the reason ARCH-311 records: a
    prompt-driven gate feeding a deterministic consumer fails silently overnight.

    Absent `created_by_kind` reads as "agent", matching the store's own default —
    an unknown author is NOT an authorization, and this and the API must agree on
    that or a deploy skew would flip the gate open.
    """
    out = []
    for it in items:
        if (it.get("type") or "") != "feature":
            out.append(it)
            continue
        if (it.get("created_by_kind") or "agent") == "human":
            out.append(it)
            continue
        # Agent-filed feature: pass only when a feature-gate decision authorizes it.
        #
        # The API nests decision_id under refs — the top-level read is a fallback
        # for a future API shape that promotes the field (issue
        # 01KYRB2J6SHDC7CSGQV6QJQQG6 — the MCP tools already hoist it, which made this bug
        # invisible when testing through the tool).
        refs = it.get("refs") or {}
        decision_id = (it.get("decision_id")
                       or (refs.get("decision_id") if isinstance(refs, dict) else "")
                       or "").strip()
        if decision_id:
            state, directive = _resolve_decision(decision_id)
            if state in ("approved", "redirected"):
                cleared = dict(it)
                cleared["_feature_gate_cleared"] = state
                if directive:
                    cleared["_feature_gate_directive"] = directive
                out.append(cleared)
                continue
            if state == "error":
                warnings.append(
                    f"{product}: holding back {it.get('id') or '?'} — "
                    f"feature-gate decision {decision_id!r} could not be resolved "
                    f"(lookup failure); re-checked on the next pass")
            else:
                warnings.append(
                    f"{product}: holding back {it.get('id') or '?'} — "
                    f"feature-gate decision {decision_id!r} is {state!r}, "
                    f"not yet approved or redirected")
            continue
        warnings.append(
            f"{product}: holding back {it.get('id') or '?'} — an agent-filed feature "
            f"is a proposal, not authorized work; it needs a feature-gate decision")
    return out


def _operator_watch() -> list[str]:
    """Surface every `needs=operator` issue, oldest first, on every pass.

    WHY THIS RUNS HERE rather than as its own scheduled job. `operator` issues are
    excluded from `claimable` by design, so no drain ever sees one and nothing
    revisits them — that exclusion is the whole point of the field and also its
    failure mode. This command already runs nightly with the queue in hand, so the
    re-read costs one extra API call and inherits the cadence for free.

    WHY THE ASYMMETRY MAKES THIS WORTH DOING. Mis-filing agent-possible work as
    `agent` is loud: a drain claims it, fails, and someone re-files it (that is how
    issue_01KYN163D85VMWHF66JXAFAEP0 got corrected). Mis-filing it as `operator` is
    silent: no drain sees it, no failure fires, and the item waits on a human who is
    waiting on the queue. issue_01KYGK70A6Y9GFHE8P0DJZ3KJA sat that way for two days
    — declined because the work "needs an operator action in the Render dashboard",
    which was true of the dashboard and false of the Render API an agent already had
    a key for.

    WHY IT DOES NOT TRY TO CLASSIFY. The first version of this scanned each body for
    venue words ("dashboard", "console") absent capability words ("credential",
    "no agent"), and flagged the mismatch as probably-misclassified. It was tested
    against the live queue and did not flag the one item known to be wrong:
    issue_01KYQ44ZYB4DA5B7YYNJWGEPTT says "requires the Render dashboard, which no
    agent can reach", and "no agent" reads as a capability claim even though the
    claim is false — the platform holds a Render API key that can do it.

    Telling a true "no agent can reach this" from a false one is semantic, not
    lexical, so the heuristic silently passed exactly the case it was written for.
    A check that misses its motivating example while looking like a check is worse
    than no check: it converts an unknown into a false all-clear. It was removed rather than tuned, because any lexical version has the
    same shape.

    What is left is the thing that actually carries signal — these items exist, here
    they are, and here is how long each has waited. Age is the honest proxy: an
    operator issue open for weeks is worth re-reading whatever its wording, and a
    human or a later pass can judge what a regex cannot.
    """
    out: list[str] = []
    try:
        items = _get("/api/issues", {"state": "open", "needs": "operator"})
    except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 — a watch must never fail the drain
        return [f"could not read the operator queue ({exc}); "
                f"nothing on it was surfaced this pass"]
    if not items:
        return out

    def _age_days(it: dict) -> float:
        # TypeError as well as ValueError. A timestamp with no timezone designator
        # ("2026-07-01T00:00:00") parses FINE and yields a naive datetime; the
        # subtraction from an aware `now()` is what raises, one line later. Catching
        # only the parse error would let that escape through `items.sort()` and take
        # down the drain this watch rides on — the one thing it must never do.
        raw = (it.get("created_at") or "").replace("Z", "+00:00")
        try:
            return (dt.datetime.now(dt.timezone.utc)
                    - dt.datetime.fromisoformat(raw)).total_seconds() / 86400
        except (ValueError, TypeError):
            return -1.0

    items.sort(key=_age_days, reverse=True)
    out.append(f"{len(items)} issue(s) waiting on a human (needs=operator), excluded "
               f"from this drain by design — re-read each against what an agent can "
               f"actually reach, and re-file as `agent` or `either` if nothing "
               f"specific is missing:")
    for it in items:
        age = _age_days(it)
        age_s = f"{age:.0f}d" if age >= 0 else "  ?"
        out.append(f"  {age_s:>4}  {it.get('id')} [{it.get('product')}] "
                   f"{(it.get('title') or '')[:84]}")
    return out


def _review_round_cap() -> str:
    """The review-round cap sentence to state in the brief: `review_rounds_code` and
    `review_rounds_per_role` from the policy file at the knowledge repo's freshly
    fetched origin/main, with the fallbacks when the file is missing, unreadable or
    breaks a rule. Mirrors `registry.limits`'s read of the same file."""
    try:
        policy = AP.load(AP.policy_text_at_origin_main(str(KNOWLEDGE_REPO)))
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - any failure here must fall back, not raise
        policy = AP.Fallback("review-round cap read raised an exception")
    return AP.review_round_cap_sentence(policy)


def _task_for(product: str, count: int, max_issues: int,
              types: set[str] | None = None,
              issues: list[dict] | None = None,
              *, work_dir: pathlib.Path) -> str:
    """The orchestrator's brief — `issue-drain`'s, with the queue query corrected
    to `claimable` and an explicit per-run bound.

    `max_issues=0` means drain the whole product queue. The bound sentence has to
    change with it rather than interpolating a 0: an agent told to "WORK AT MOST 0
    ISSUES" reasonably does nothing, and the pass would report success having
    drained nothing at all.

    `issues` carries the authorized-feature list so that any pre-approved or
    redirected feature issues get named in the brief with their directives. The
    orchestrator's step 3 defaults to escalating feature/task issues; the
    AUTHORIZED FEATURES section below is the override for the ones the drain
    already cleared through the feature gate.

    `work_dir` is the directory the drain created for this dispatch. The orchestrator
    writes every filer-text file into it, so no file name is predictable in /tmp.
    """
    cap_sentence = _review_round_cap()
    if max_issues:
        bound = (
            f"2. WORK AT MOST {max_issues} ISSUES THIS RUN, oldest first. Each issue "
            f"costs a bug-fixer plus two reviewers, so this bound is what keeps a "
            f"nightly pass from becoming an open-ended spend. Stop when you reach it; "
            f"the rest are picked up on the next pass. Do not raise the bound "
            f"yourself.\n\n")
    else:
        bound = (
            f"2. WORK EVERY CLAIMABLE ISSUE ON THIS PRODUCT, oldest first — there were "
            f"{count} when this run started. The operator's call (2026-07-27) is that the drain "
            f"clears the queue rather than nibbling at it, so there is no per-run cap. "
            f"Do not stop early because the queue looks long, and do not invent a cap "
            f"of your own. If you genuinely cannot finish one, record its outcome and "
            f"move to the next; an issue left claimed with no outcome is the one "
            f"failure this run cannot recover from.\n\n")
    scope = ""
    if types:
        scope = (
            f"3a. THIS RUN IS SCOPED TO TYPE {'/'.join(sorted(types))}. Issues of any "
            f"other type are deliberately out of scope for this pass: skip them, leave "
            f"them in the queue untouched, and do NOT claim them. They are being "
            f"handled separately.\n\n")
    # Build the authorized-features override for issues that cleared the gate.
    # These are agent-filed features with an approved or redirected decision; step
    # 3 would escalate them, but they must be drained to a fix PR instead.
    cleared = [
        (it.get("id", "?"), it.get("title", ""),
         it.get("_feature_gate_cleared", "approved"),
         it.get("_feature_gate_directive", ""))
        for it in (issues or [])
        if it.get("_feature_gate_cleared")
    ]
    if cleared:
        lines = "\n".join(
            (f"   - {iid} \"{title}\": redirected — the operator's directive supersedes the "
             f"original proposal: {directive!r}")
            if directive
            else f"   - {iid} \"{title}\": approved — build the original proposal."
            for iid, title, gate_state, directive in cleared
        )
        authorized_section = (
            "AUTHORIZED FEATURES — EXCEPTION TO STEP 3: The following feature issues "
            "carry an approved feature-gate decision. Drain them to a fix PR "
            "(steps 4-9) instead of escalating. The directive, when present, IS the "
            "spec — build exactly what it says, not the original proposal:\n"
            + lines + "\n\n")
    else:
        authorized_section = ""
    # The identity this brief checks out, releases and links under. The ops token is shared
    # by every session, so the Library tells holders apart by this string alone. It names
    # this dispatch: the product, and the work_dir mkdtemp made unique for it.
    owner = f"issue-queue-drain:{product}:{work_dir.name}"
    return (
        f"Task: Drain the in-box issue queue for {product} end-to-end. The queue is "
        f"/issues on team.convivy.com (ARCH-359/360), read and updated through the ops "
        f"MCP tools (mcp__ops__issue_*). This is the unattended nightly drain, so "
        f"nobody is watching: every issue you touch must end in a recorded state.\n\n"
        f"1. List the issues to work: mcp__ops__issue_list(product=\"{product}\", "
        f"state=\"claimable\"). Use state=\"claimable\", NOT \"open\" — claimable is "
        f"open plus issues whose 24h claim TTL has expired (ARCH-361 Part B), so a "
        f"previous run that died does not strand its issues out of the queue forever. "
        f"The queue never returns the operator's \"Won't do\" issues, so you skip those "
        f"naturally. There were {count} claimable when this run started.\n\n"
        + bound + scope +
        # ARCH-361's drain routing existed as documentation ONLY: control_db defines
        # ISSUE_AUTO_FIX_TYPES, issue_api's module docstring says non-auto-fix types
        # route to /engineering/decisions, and no code anywhere reads either. Both
        # drains omitted it, so a `task` would have been auto-fixed. The operator's call
        # (2026-07-26) is that the documented rule is the right one, so the brief is
        # where it finally gets implemented.
        "3. ROUTE ON THE ISSUE'S `type` BEFORE DOING ANYTHING ELSE WITH IT.\n\n"
        "   - type bug, nit, or improvement -> drain it to a fix PR (steps 4-9 below).\n"
        "   - type task or feature -> do NOT build it. It needs the operator's scoping call, "
        "which this unattended run cannot make. Record the blocker durably with "
        "mcp__ops__signal_log(kind=\"needs-decision\", product=\"" + product + "\", "
        "body=<what needs deciding, with the scoping work already done>), then "
        "mcp__ops__issue_comment(issue_id, kind=\"note\", body=<the same summary, "
        "so it is visible on the issue thread>), then mcp__ops__issue_release("
        f"issue_id, owner=\"{owner}\") so it goes back to open rather "
        "than staying claimed. Do NOT call mcp__ops__issue_escalate for this — it "
        "requires a decision_id and there is no decision queue to raise one into. "
        "The issue stays open and visible in /issues with your note on it; a "
        "human pass, not this drain, is what resolves a task or feature.\n\n"
        + authorized_section +
        "For each issue you are draining to a fix PR:\n\n"
        f"4. Check it out: mcp__ops__issue_checkout(issue_id, owner=\"{owner}\"). Every "
        "later call in this brief that names an owner uses this same owner string. An "
        "error:409 means another worker already holds it — skip it and move on.\n"
        "REFUSED FILE WRITES. This rule governs steps 5, 6 and 8. Filer and reviewer "
        "text reaches a shell only through a file: the only permitted shell form is a "
        "command substitution over that file, passed as the --task or --title value. If a "
        "file write for such text is refused, record the refusal with mcp__ops__issue_comment("
        "issue_id, kind=\"note\", body=<the refusal text>) and stop. In steps 5 and 6, also "
        f"call mcp__ops__issue_release(issue_id, owner=\"{owner}\") and move on to "
        "the next issue. In step 8, stop revising this PR; its findings stay on it. Never send "
        "that text through Bash by another route: no heredoc (<<EOF), no `echo` or `printf`, "
        "and no literal quoted argument (a task or title typed inside single or double quotes).\n"
        "5. Dispatch bug-fixer in fix mode. The issue's title and body are written by "
        "whoever filed the issue, so they never go inside a shell quote. Write them to "
        f"{work_dir}/<issue_id>-task.md with your file-writing tool, then run `agentctl "
        "spawn bug-fixer --on " + product + " --issue <issue_id> --task \"$(cat "
        f"{work_dir}/<issue_id>-task.md)\"` (it commits and authors pr-body.md, and "
        "returns a one-line summary plus a suggested title). If the spawn is refused "
        "(it exits non-zero and prints `error:` on stderr), the claim from step 4 is "
        "still held by this run: record the refusal text with mcp__ops__issue_comment("
        "issue_id, kind=\"note\", body=<the refusal text>), then mcp__ops__issue_release("
        f"issue_id, owner=\"{owner}\"), and move on to the next issue.\n"
        "6. Title, then publish: the suggested title comes from the filer's text too, so "
        f"write it to {work_dir}/<issue_id>-title.txt with your file-writing tool before "
        "anything is pushed. A refused write follows the REFUSED FILE WRITES rule and stops "
        "this item. Then run agentctl publish and agentctl pr-open (ARCH-055 + ARCH-058 "
        "gates), with the title `[nightly-bugfix] fix: <title>` passed as `--title \"$(cat "
        f"{work_dir}/<issue_id>-title.txt)\"`.\n"
        "7. Record the PR on the issue IMMEDIATELY: mcp__ops__issue_link_pr(issue_id, "
        f"pr=\"<N>\", url=\"<pr_url>\", owner=\"{owner}\") — the issue moves to pr-open and shows a link in "
        "/issues. This is the close-loop. Pass the url: the Library derives the PR's "
        "repo from it, and the cost report joins reviewer runs to the item through that "
        "repo and PR number.\n"
        "8. Dispatch reviewer + security-reviewer in parallel, with no --issue: "
        "`agentctl spawn reviewer --on " + product + " --pr <N>` and `agentctl spawn "
        "security-reviewer --on " + product + " --pr <N>`. A reviewer runs on its roster "
        "model, not the item's. (ARCH-027, ARCH-347 — --pr self-pins the live head; "
        "--pr now REQUIRES --on naming the repo, or spawn refuses.) If a reviewer "
        "dispatch is refused, re-dispatch that reviewer once; if the second attempt is "
        "refused too, record the PR as unreviewed in your final report and move on. "
        "Auto-cycle on any finding or red CI: write the findings verbatim to "
        f"{work_dir}/<issue_id>-findings-<round>.md with your file-writing tool, where "
        "<round> is this revise round's number, counting from 1, then re-dispatch "
        "bug-fixer: `agentctl spawn bug-fixer --on " + product + " --pr <N> --issue "
        f"<issue_id> --task \"$(cat {work_dir}/<issue_id>-findings-<round>.md)\"`, re-publish "
        "with --pr <N>, re-review. If that revise dispatch is refused, record the refusal on "
        "the issue thread with mcp__ops__issue_comment and stop revising this PR; its "
        "findings stay on the PR. A refused file write for this text follows the REFUSED "
        "FILE WRITES rule above. "
        f"{cap_sentence} A finding that repeats one an earlier round raised, in a round past the document cap, "
        "ends the cycle: a headless run dispatches no further round, leaves the PR pr-open, and records "
        "the repeat on the issue thread with mcp__ops__issue_comment.\n"
        "9. Terminal state is a ready-to-merge PR. Per ARCH-020 do NOT merge — the operator "
        "merges. The issue stays pr-open; a later in-box merge marks it fixed. When the "
        f"pass ends, remove the directory with `rm -rf \"{work_dir}\"`: it holds the "
        "filer's text. The drain does not remove it after a detached dispatch, which "
        "returns before the pass ends.\n\n"
        "If an issue needs the operator's call rather than a fix (a design decision, an "
        "ambiguous spec): mcp__ops__signal_log(kind=\"needs-decision\", "
        "product=\"" + product + "\", body=<what needs deciding>), then "
        "mcp__ops__issue_comment(issue_id, kind=\"note\", body=<the same summary>), "
        f"then mcp__ops__issue_release(issue_id, owner=\"{owner}\") so "
        "it goes back to open with your note on the thread rather than staying "
        "claimed.\n\n"
        "IMPORTANT — never leave a claimed issue with no recorded outcome. Once you "
        "claim an issue you own recording where it went (pr-open / released-with-a-"
        "note / resolved). A claimed issue with no outcome is invisible to the next "
        "pass until its 24h TTL expires. So if bug-fixer FAILS to produce a PR (it "
        f"errors, or hits the review-round cap in step 8 before a PR opens), do not move on silently "
        "— record what's stuck with signal_log and issue_comment as above, then "
        f"mcp__ops__issue_release(issue_id, owner=\"{owner}\"), so it surfaces in /issues "
        "for the operator rather than staying "
        f"stuck-claimed. At the step 8 cap for that PR's kind AFTER a PR opened, leave it pr-open; its "
        "findings are on the PR. Per ARCH-030 verify every code-specific claim.\n\n"
        "Do NOT run `mark-fresh` — that is `issue-drain`'s daytime cost-shift "
        "bookkeeping, which tells the next overnight to skip its bug-fix phase. This "
        "nightly drain runs alongside the overnight rather than standing in for it, so "
        "suppressing a product's bug-fix phase here would be wrong."
    )


def _spawn(product: str, task: str, detach: bool, work_dir: pathlib.Path) -> bool:
    """Dispatch the orchestrator for one product. True if it started.

    Detached by default: this command runs from launchd with nothing to notify,
    and the orchestrator records its own outcomes on the issues rather than
    returning anything this caller needs to read. That is the opposite of
    changelog-drain, which runs --foreground precisely because it must have the
    entry text in hand before it can post.

    NO `--pr`, and nothing PR-scoped: an issue has no PR until the drain opens one.

    `work_dir` holds the filer's text. It is removed here when no reader is left: a
    refused spawn, or a foreground dispatch that returned. A detached dispatch that
    started keeps it for the orchestrator (brief step 9). A timed-out dispatch keeps it
    too: the kill stops agentctl, and the orchestrator it started may still be writing.
    """
    cmd = [str(AGENTCTL), "spawn", _ORCHESTRATOR, "--on", product]
    agents_dir = _agents_dir()
    if agents_dir:
        cmd += ["--agents-dir", str(agents_dir)]
    cmd += ["--task", task]
    cmd += ["--detach"] if detach else ["--foreground"]
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=1800)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        # One stuck dispatch is one failure, not a lost pass — the same lesson
        # changelog-drain's review recorded (#250). Uncaught, this would abort the
        # loop and every product behind it would be skipped with no account.
        print(f"  {product}: dispatch exceeded 30m and was killed", file=sys.stderr)
        return False
    if proc.returncode != 0 or not detach:
        shutil.rmtree(work_dir, ignore_errors=True)
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        detail = (proc.stderr or proc.stdout).strip().splitlines()
        print(f"  {product}: dispatch failed — {detail[-1] if detail else 'no output'}",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return False
    out = (proc.stdout or "") + (proc.stderr or "")
    for tok in out.split():
        if tok.startswith("run-"):
            print(f"  dispatched {product} ({tok})", flush=True)
            return True
    print(f"  dispatched {product}", flush=True)
    return True


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="issue-queue-drain", description=__doc__)
    ap.add_argument("--product", help="drain only this product")
    ap.add_argument("--max-products", type=int, default=0,
                    help="dispatch at most this many products in one pass "
                         "(0 = every product, the default)")
    ap.add_argument("--max-issues", type=int, default=0,
                    help="issues each product's orchestrator may work "
                         "(0 = every claimable issue, the default)")
    ap.add_argument("--type", dest="types",
                    help="comma-separated issue types to drain (bug,nit,improvement,"
                         "task,epic); default is every type")
    ap.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true",
                    help="print the plan and dispatch nothing")
    ap.add_argument("--foreground", action="store_true",
                    help="run each dispatch attached, for watching one live")
    args = ap.parse_args(argv if argv is not None else sys.argv[1:])

    types = None
    if args.types:
        types = {t.strip() for t in args.types.split(",") if t.strip()}
        unknown = types - _ISSUE_TYPES
        if unknown:
            print(f"issue-queue-drain: unknown --type {','.join(sorted(unknown))}; "
                  f"valid types are {','.join(sorted(_ISSUE_TYPES))}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2

    by_product, warnings = _claimable(types)
    for w in warnings:
        print(f"issue-queue-drain: WARNING — {w}", file=sys.stderr)

    # BEFORE the --product early return, not after. An operator issue is invisible
    # to every other part of this command, so the one thing that must not be
    # conditional is saying it exists. Placed after the early return, a
    # `--product X` run against an empty queue returned before reporting operator
    # issues for ANY product — the same silent-drop this watch exists to end.
    for line in _operator_watch():
        print(f"issue-queue-drain: {line}", file=sys.stderr)

    if args.product:
        by_product = {k: v for k, v in by_product.items() if k == args.product}
        if not by_product:
            print(f"issue-queue-drain: no claimable issues for {args.product}",
                  flush=True)
            return 0

    total = sum(len(v) for v in by_product.values())
    print(f"issue-queue-drain: {total} claimable issue(s) across "
          f"{len(by_product)} product(s)", flush=True)

    # Most issues first: the deepest queue is where a bounded pass buys the most.
    ordered = sorted(by_product.items(), key=lambda kv: (-len(kv[1]), kv[0]))

    # Both skip classes are reported BY NAME. A product with no primary checkout
    # is skipped on every pass forever, and a count with no names hides which —
    # today's lesson is that a queue with no consumer looks exactly like an
    # empty one.
    undispatchable = [(p, len(v)) for p, v in ordered if not _dispatchable(p)]
    if undispatchable:
        print(f"issue-queue-drain: skipping {len(undispatchable)} product(s) with no "
              f"primary checkout agentctl can dispatch against: "
              f"{', '.join(f'{p} ({n})' for p, n in undispatchable)}", file=sys.stderr)
    ordered = [(p, v) for p, v in ordered if _dispatchable(p)]

    plan = []
    for product, issues in ordered:
        if args.max_products and len(plan) >= args.max_products:
            break
        plan.append((product, issues))

    deferred = [p for p, _ in ordered if p not in {q for q, _ in plan}]
    if deferred:
        print(f"issue-queue-drain: deferring {len(deferred)} product(s) to the next "
              f"pass (--max-products {args.max_products}): {', '.join(deferred)}",
              file=sys.stderr)

    if args.dry_run:
        for product, issues in plan:
            bound = (f"up to {args.max_issues}" if args.max_issues
                     else f"all {len(issues)}")
            print(f"  would drain {product}: {len(issues)} claimable, "
                  f"{bound} this run", flush=True)
        return 0

    started = failed = 0
    for i, (product, issues) in enumerate(plan):
        # WAVE, don't fan out (ARCH-422). This loop used to dispatch every product
        # at once. On 2026-07-29 that put nine planning-orchestrators into the
        # 16-slot registry, so nine slots were held by supervisors all night and
        # every worker in the fleet fought over the remaining seven. constance's
        # drain opened PR #555 and was then refused a reviewer nine times over ~55
        # minutes; under draft-gated CI (ARCH-364) that PR could never run CI and
        # could never merge. The starvation stranded work rather than delaying it.
        #
        # `_wait_for_supervisor_slot` blocks until the registry will admit another
        # supervisor. The registry enforces the cap either way, so without this the
        # spawn would simply be REFUSED and the product silently dropped from the
        # night — trading a starvation bug for a coverage bug, which is worse
        # because it looks like an empty queue.
        # Unconditional, including the first product. The first draft skipped the
        # wait for i==0 on the reasoning that nothing is holding a slot yet — true
        # only when the cap starts empty, which is an assumption rather than a
        # guarantee. An overnight stage agent plus three planning-orchestrators from
        # an earlier pass puts the cap at its limit before this loop begins, and
        # product 0 would then fail immediately while products 1..N correctly
        # waited. When a slot is free this returns on its first check, so waiting
        # unconditionally costs nothing in the common case.
        if not _wait_for_supervisor_slot():
            print(f"issue-queue-drain: WARNING — timed out waiting for a supervisor "
                  f"slot; {len(plan) - i} product(s) NOT dispatched this pass: "
                  f"{', '.join(p for p, _ in plan[i:])}. They stay claimable and the "
                  f"next pass picks them up.", flush=True)
            break
        # mkdtemp makes a fresh 0700 directory, so no brief file name is predictable in
        # /tmp and no file from an earlier night is there for the Write tool to refuse.
        work_dir = pathlib.Path(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="issue-queue-drain-"))
        ok = _spawn(product, _task_for(product, len(issues), args.max_issues, types, issues,
                                       work_dir=work_dir),
                    detach=not args.foreground, work_dir=work_dir)
        if ok:
            started += 1
        else:
            failed += 1

    print(f"issue-queue-drain: dispatched {started}, failed {failed}", flush=True)
    return 1 if failed else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(main())
